WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.303
A bicycle rental application remembers an unfinished
booking in worker A’s memory.

00:00:05.303 --> 00:00:10.412
The customer’s next request reaches worker B, which
has never seen the booking.

00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:16.879
Both workers run identical code, but they are not
interchangeable for this interaction because their

00:00:16.879 --> 00:00:18.754
retained information differs.

00:00:18.754 --> 00:00:24.833
Calling the protocol HTTP does not remove that
dependency: application state can exist above a

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:26.579
stateless request protocol.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.240
Identify the information needed for the next
operation and locate its authoritative copy.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.487
A stateless worker may still read changing data and
perform writes.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:42.824
For example, it can accept a booking identifier,
load that booking from shared storage and update it

00:00:42.824 --> 00:00:43.205
there.

00:00:43.205 --> 00:00:49.035
The worker does not need the customer’s previous
requests to have visited that same process.

00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:54.597
This distinction separates deployment statelessness
from a mathematical pure function.

00:00:54.597 --> 00:00:59.059
To test your design, replace the worker between
consecutive requests.

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:05.332
If another healthy worker can continue using the
request and shared services, the application has

00:01:05.332 --> 00:01:08.695
avoided a hidden dependency on the original process.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:14.663
Move the bicycle booking into shared storage, and a
replacement worker can retrieve it after a

00:01:14.663 --> 00:01:15.167
restart.

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:21.284
That improves replaceability but exposes a second
question: what if two workers reserve the final

00:01:21.284 --> 00:01:22.609
bicycle concurrently?

00:01:22.609 --> 00:01:28.284
If both read availability before either writes, each
may believe the reservation is valid.

00:01:28.284 --> 00:01:34.913
Shared storage solves access to a common record; a
suitable atomic operation or version check must

00:01:34.913 --> 00:01:36.942
also protect the booking rule.

00:01:36.942 --> 00:01:42.894
For example, worker A reads booking version 12,
while worker B changes it to version 13.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:49.591
A conditional update based on version 12 should now
fail rather than silently overwrite B’s change.

00:01:49.591 --> 00:01:52.976
The application can reload and explain the conflict.

00:01:52.976 --> 00:01:59.420
A session cookie can identify the customer whose
request is being processed, but it does not itself

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.373
enforce inventory correctness.

00:02:01.373 --> 00:02:07.557
Explain these responsibilities separately: the
request carries identity or a session reference,

00:02:07.557 --> 00:02:13.806
shared storage retains durable state, and the update
protocol decides which concurrent change is

00:02:13.806 --> 00:02:14.392
accepted.
